---
import LegalLayout from '../../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { getLegalPage, legalHref } from '../../lib/legal';
import { SITE } from '../../lib/seo';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/paths';

const page = getLegalPage('disclaimer')!;
---

<LegalLayout
	page={page}
	intro="Please read this disclaimer alongside the Terms of Use and Copyright Policy."
>
	<div class="callout">
		<p>
			This disclaimer explains the independent and educational nature of Digital Catholic. It is
			<strong>not</strong> permission to publish inaccurate Catholic doctrine, and it does not reduce
			our responsibility to correct errors when they are identified.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>1. Independent resource</h2>
	<p>
		Digital Catholic is an <strong>independent</strong> Catholic educational and devotional resource. It
		is not an official publication of the Holy See, the Vatican, a diocese, a parish, a religious order,
		or any other ecclesiastical authority, unless a specific affiliation is expressly stated and verified
		on the relevant page.
	</p>

	<h2>2. No implied ecclesiastical endorsement</h2>
	<p>
		References to Scripture, the Catechism, councils, popes, saints, apparitions, or liturgical practice
		do not imply that any Church authority has reviewed, approved, or endorsed this Website as a whole.
		Church status labels on individual articles (where present) reflect only what that article reports
		about a particular event or devotion.
	</p>

	<h2>3. Educational and devotional purpose</h2>
	<p>
		Content is offered to support learning, prayer, catechesis, and informed curiosity about the Catholic
		faith. It is written to be accessible, not to replace formal theological study or sacramental life in
		the Church.
	</p>

	<h2>4. Not a substitute for Church teaching or pastoral care</h2>
	<p>
		Nothing on Digital Catholic replaces the Magisterium of the Catholic Church, the guidance of your
		bishop or parish priest, the Sacrament of Reconciliation, spiritual direction, or professional advice
		(including legal, medical, or counselling advice). For matters of conscience, doctrine, or pastoral
		need, consult competent Church authority and, where appropriate, qualified professionals.
	</p>

	<h2>5. Scripture and doctrinal references</h2>
	<p>
		Scripture citations, Catechism paragraph numbers, and summaries of doctrine should be checked against
		authoritative editions and translations approved for your region. Brief quotations and paraphrases on
		this site are aids to reading, not critical editions.
	</p>

	<h2>6. History, apparitions, and miracles</h2>
	<p>
		Historical narratives and reports of apparitions or miracles may carry different levels of
		ecclesiastical recognition. Where articles include a Church status note, read it carefully. The Church
		does not require belief in private revelations. Always distinguish public revelation and defined
		doctrine from private revelation and pious tradition.
	</p>

	<h2>7. Liturgical calendars and local practice</h2>
	<p>
		Liturgical calendars, feast ranks, readings, and customs can vary by rite, particular calendar, and
		local diocesan practice. Information on this Website may follow the General Roman Calendar or other
		stated conventions and may not match every parish schedule.
	</p>

	<h2>8. Accuracy and currency</h2>
	<p>
		We aim for accuracy and reverence, but we do not guarantee that every page is complete, error-free, or
		up to date. Theology, historical scholarship, and liturgical norms can be nuanced; summaries may omit
		detail.
	</p>

	<h2>9. Corrections welcome</h2>
	<p>
		If you find an error or have a doctrinal, historical, or attribution concern, please tell us using the
		<a href={legalHref('corrections')}>Content Corrections &amp; Copyright Requests</a> procedure or the
		<a href={withBase('/contact')}>Contact</a> page
		(<a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>).
	</p>
</LegalLayout>
